The `\breathe` command calls for the performer to shorten the previous note to take a breath.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\fixed c'' { c2. \breathe d4 }
}
}
```

For a short break in sound that is not taken away from the previous note, “Caesuras”.

Unlike other expressive marks, a breath mark is treated as a separate music event; therefore, any expressive marks pertaining to the preceding note, and any brackets indicating manual beams, slurs, or phrasing slurs, must be placed before `\breathe`. `\breathe` does not accept articulations itself, but “Caesuras”.

A breath mark ends an automatic beam; to override this, “Manual beams”.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\fixed c'' { c8 \breathe d e f g2 }
}
}
```

The `breathMarkType` context property controls which of several predefined breath marks the `\breathe` command creates.  “List of breath marks”.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\fixed c'' {
  \set breathMarkType = #'tickmark
  c2. \breathe d4
}
}
}
```

**See also:** Music Glossary: “breath mark” (Music Glossary).

Notation Reference: “Caesuras”, “Divisiones”.

Snippets: “Expressive marks” (snippets).

Internals Reference: `BreathingEvent` (Internals Reference), `BreathingSign` (Internals Reference), `Breathing_sign_engraver` (Internals Reference).

Short trills without an extender line are printed with `\trill`; see “Articulations and ornamentations”.

Longer trills with an extender line are made with `\startTrillSpan` and `\stopTrillSpan`:

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  d''1\startTrillSpan
  d1
  c2\stopTrillSpan
  r2
}
}
}
```

A trill spanner crossing a line break will restart exactly above the first note on the new line.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  d''1\startTrillSpan
  \break
  d1
  c2\stopTrillSpan
  r2
}
}
}
```

Consecutive trill spans will work without explicit `\stopTrillSpan` commands, since successive trill spanners will automatically become the right bound of the previous trill.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  d''1\startTrillSpan
  d1
  b1\startTrillSpan
  d2\stopTrillSpan
  r2
}
}
}
```

Trills can also be combined with grace notes.  The syntax of this construct and the method to precisely position the grace notes are described in “Grace notes”.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  d''1~\afterGrace
  d1\startTrillSpan { c32[ d]\stopTrillSpan }
  c2 r2
}
}
}
```

Trills that require an auxiliary note with an explicit pitch can be typeset with the `\pitchedTrill` command.  The first argument is the main note, and the second is the *trilled* note, printed as a stemless note head in parentheses.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  \pitchedTrill
  d''2\startTrillSpan fis
  d2
  c2\stopTrillSpan
  r2
}
}
}
```

The Accidental of the first pitched trill in a measure is always printed, even for naturals.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative c' {
{
  \key d \major
  \pitchedTrill
  d'2\startTrillSpan cis d\stopTrillSpan
  \pitchedTrill
  d2\startTrillSpan c d\stopTrillSpan
  \pitchedTrill
  d2\startTrillSpan e d\stopTrillSpan
}
}
}
}
```

Subsequent accidentals (of the same note in the same measure) will need to be added manually.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  \pitchedTrill
  eis''4\startTrillSpan fis
  eis4\stopTrillSpan
  \pitchedTrill
  eis4\startTrillSpan cis
  eis4\stopTrillSpan
  \pitchedTrill
  eis4\startTrillSpan fis
  eis4\stopTrillSpan
  \pitchedTrill
  eis4\startTrillSpan fis!
  eis4\stopTrillSpan
}
}
}
```

**Predefined commands:** `\startTrillSpan`, `\stopTrillSpan`.

**See also:** Music Glossary: “trill” (Music Glossary).

