Hidden (or invisible or transparent) notes can be useful in preparing theory or composition exercises.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative {
  c''4 d
  \hideNotes
  e4 f
  \unHideNotes
  g a
  \hideNotes
  b
  \unHideNotes
  c
}
}
}
```

Note heads, stems, and flags, and rests are invisible.  Beams are invisible if they start on a hidden note.  Objects that are attached to invisible notes are still visible.

Example (renders in Score Chat):
```lilypond
\version "2.24.4"
\score {
\new Staff {
\relative c'' {
  e8(\p f g a)--
  \hideNotes
  e8(\p f g a)--
}
}
}
```

**Predefined commands:** `\hideNotes`, `\unHideNotes`.

**See also:** Learning Manual: “Visibility and color of objects” (Learning Manual).

